<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Comtat Venaissin</h1><p class="st-meta">1274 CE – 1791 CE · capital Carpentras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A papal territory in Provence, governed from Carpentras for five centuries, where the popes lived at nearby Avignon in the 14th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Comtat had belonged to the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">Toulouse</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1229 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vii-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VII</a> ceded it to the Church, but the French crown held it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III</a> handed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-x">Pope Gregory X</a> in 1274.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Comtat was governed by a papal rector at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpentras">Carpentras</a>, with its own estates. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> moved to neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> in 1309 and bought the city in 1348, and the region flourished during the Avignon papacy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> found refuge there as the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comtadin-jews">Pope's Jews</a>', living in communities at Carpentras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavaillon">Cavaillon</a>, Avignon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisle-sur-la-sorgue">L'Isle-sur-la-Sorgue</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied the territory several times in disputes with the popes. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">Revolution</a>, after local votes, the National Constituent Assembly annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and the Comtat to France in 1791; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> accepted the loss in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tolentino">Treaty of Tolentino</a> in 1797.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/comtat-venaissin.jpg" alt="Comtat Venaissin" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original file: User at openclipart.org, File:Flag of Comtat Venaissin.svg (Open Clip Art).svg Modified: Superben... · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Comtat_Venaissin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1274 CE – 1791 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carpentras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gregory X, Clement VI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Venaissin, Papal Avignon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=comtat-venaissin" aria-label="Comtat Venaissin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4801 618 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Comtat Venaissin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5121 874L5304 942M5121 874L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5121 874h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5121 874h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Carpentras. <a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=comtat-venaissin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

